From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 04:56:31 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: Check a name's drive and elements in Windows' order.

Windows looks at nothing after a DOS drive that does not exist: on
Windows 11, \??\Q:\x, \??\Q:\a:b\c, \??\Q:\.\x and \??\::\x (the
drive "::" that a Win32 path "::C:\x" names) all fail with
STATUS_OBJECT_PATH_NOT_FOUND.  Wine skips checking a drive to save a
stat call, and answered from the rest of the name instead; it now
checks the drive when the lookup fails.

On a drive that exists, an empty element or a stream on the way is
refused at once, but an element with an invalid character, or ".."
or ".", only when the lookup reaches it, so a missing directory
before it makes the path missing; "." on the way is a missing path
itself, as Windows 11 answers (the tests took the other answer for
the broken one).  tools/colonpathprobe in wine-altars has the
measurements.

The four mfplat source resolver tests that open "::<path>" pass, and
so do the tests for a link target with "." on the way and for a DOS
drive that has been removed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

